/-
# Involution classes with products of odd order (Kourovka Notebook Problem 10.62)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 10.62 (A. I. Sozutov), marked solved: construct an example of a
(periodic) group without subgroups of index `2` which is generated by a conjugacy class of
involutions `X` such that the order of the product of any two involutions from `X` is odd.
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.OrderOfElement

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.InvolutionClassOddProductsKourovka10Problem62Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 10.62 (A. I. Sozutov). UnsolvedMath record
UM-00700677 (`KOU-10.62`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

"Construct" is formalized as existence, with the parenthetical "periodic" included (the constructed
group is periodic). `X` is the conjugacy class of an involution `x`, and the condition is required
for all `y, z ∈ X`, including `y = z` (order `1`). No finite group is an example: by Glauberman's
`Z*`-theorem it would have a subgroup of index `2`.
-/

/-- Kourovka 10.62 (A. I. Sozutov): some periodic group without subgroups of index `2` is generated
by a conjugacy class `X` of involutions such that the product of any two members of `X` has odd
order. -/
def periodicGroupGeneratedByOddInvolutionClass : Prop :=
  ∃ (G : Type) (_ : Group G), (∀ g : G, IsOfFinOrder g) ∧ (∀ H : Subgroup G, H.index ≠ 2) ∧
    ∃ x : G, orderOf x = 2 ∧ Subgroup.closure {y | IsConj x y} = ⊤ ∧
      ∀ y z : G, IsConj x y → IsConj x z → Odd (orderOf (y * z))

/--
Resolved true: Kinyon and Urban's 2026 preprint (an AI-generated solution confirmed by M. Amelio;
Section 3.7, Proposition 3.5) constructs, for every sufficiently large odd prime `p`, an infinite
three-generated perfect group of exponent `2p` whose involutions form one conjugacy class generating
the group, the product of any two distinct involutions having order `p`; being perfect, it has no
subgroup of index `2`. The Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) marks 10.62 solved, "Example constructed".
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 10.62 (A. I. Sozutov); resolution: M.
Kinyon and J. Urban (with Codex gpt-6-astra), Results on problems from the Kourovka Notebook,
preprint (2026), Section 3.7, Proposition 3.5 (built on Amelio's geometric construction); AI
solution confirmed by M. Amelio; the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) marks 10.62 solved,
https://kourovkanotebookorg.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/kourovka-mathematics.pdf.
-/
public theorem_wanted periodicGroupGeneratedByOddInvolutionClass_holds :
    periodicGroupGeneratedByOddInvolutionClass

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.InvolutionClassOddProductsKourovka10Problem62Wanted
